.TH col "" "Command"
.SH NAME
col \- remove reverse and half line motions
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcol [\-bdfx] [\-p\fIn\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
col -- remove reverse and half line motions
Usage:	col [ -bdfx ] [ -pn ]
Options:
	-b	Output device cannot backspace
	-d	Double spaced output
	-f	Output device can handle half lines (has precedence over -d)
	-pn	Set page buffer to n lines (default: 128)
	-x	Suppress conversion of white space to tabs on output
.HE
.B col
reads the standard input and writes the standard output.
It removes reverse and half-line motions (as produced by
.BR nroff )
by keeping an image of the page in memory and performing those
motions against that image, so that only forward motion appears on
the output.
.PP
.B col
recognizes the following characters and escape sequences in its
input: newline, carriage return, backspace, and tab carry their
usual meaning; the escape character (033) followed by
.BR 7 ,
.BR 8 ,
.BR 9 ,
or
.B B
is taken as a full reverse line feed, half reverse line feed, half
forward line feed, or full forward line feed, respectively; the
control character
.B VT
(013) is an alternate form of full reverse line feed; the control
characters
.B SO
(017) and
.B SI
(016) mark the start and end of an alternate character set, which
.B col
remembers per character and reproduces with matching
.B SO
/
.B SI
pairs on output.
An escape character followed by any other character is discarded
along with that character.
All other control characters are discarded.
.PP
When two or more characters are destined for the same output
position (an overstrike),
.B col
remembers all of them and, unless
.B \-b
is given, writes them as a backspace-separated overstrike sequence.
Runs of spaces on output are entabbed to every eighth column unless
.B \-x
is given.
.IP \fB\-b\fR
The output device cannot backspace.
Only the last character destined for a given position is written;
earlier overstruck characters are discarded.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Double-space the output: where the input has no intervening
half line, print two newlines instead of one.
Overridden by
.BR \-f .
.IP \fB\-f\fR
The output device can handle forward half-line motions, so
.B col
writes half lines as physical half-line motions instead of folding
them onto full lines.
Takes precedence over
.BR \-d .
.IP \fB\-p\fIn\fR
Set the size of the internal page buffer to
.I n
lines (default 128).
Lines outside the buffered window are written out as the window
advances.
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Do not convert runs of spaces on output into tabs.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B nroff
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B col
writes
.B "Scrolling backwards over top of page window."
to standard error (once) if the input scrolls above the buffered
page window, and
.B "Seem to have lost Overstruck characters."
if its internal overstrike bookkeeping becomes inconsistent; both
are warnings and do not stop processing.
It prints a usage message and exits 1 if given an unrecognized
option or a bad
.B \-p
value.
.SH NOTES
Reverse motion that would move above the top of the buffered page
window is lost; increase the page buffer with
.B \-p
if this happens.
